  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   The central idea is to leverage a genetic algorithm (GA) for global exploration of the solution space, combined with a local search for refinement. This hybrid approach attempts to overcome the limitations of the parent program's multi-resolution optimization by:\n   - Using GA for broader search and escaping local optima\n   - Incorporating repair and competition mechanisms to improve solutions\n   - Combining global exploration with local refinement\n   - Maintaining a population of competitors rather than a single solution\n   - Using both tournament selection and survival of the fittest to evolve towards better solutions\n   - Generating random mutations and elite carryover to preserve best practices\n   Key improvements over the parent include:\n   - Alternative optimization method to prevent local trapping\n   - Continuous population turnover with best-of-breed selection\n   - Four distinct operation phases: repair, evaluate, selection, and mutation\n   - More natural handling of constraints and feasibility\n   The local search maintains the refined optimization techniques from the parent program.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   The objective function is to minimize:\n      C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   Under constraints:\n      0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 \u2200i, and \u2211h[i] = n/2\n   Representation:\n      Solutions are arrays of length n_points\n   Variables:\n      h: step function values\n   Fitness Function:\n      Fitness = -C5 (for GA minimization)\n   Repair Mechanism:\n      Clipping and iterative adjustment to enforce constraints\n   Operations:\n      - crossover: Dependent probability based on temperature\n      - mutation: Random walk with rejection condition\n      - repair: Maintain feasibility after changes\n   Optimizations:\n      Phase 1: GA exploration (max 300 steps)\n      Phase 2: Sub-population tournament at intermediate temperature\n      Phase 3: Local refinement using parent's local_search function\n      Phase 4: Ultra-detailed HA/EA sweeps\n   Decision variables:\n      population_size = 20\n      mutation_rate = 0.1\n      repair_runs = 200\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   - Initialize population from initial_h_values\n   - Set up repair and objective functions\n   - Create generation loop with cooling schedule\n   - For each generation:\n        1. Evaluate fitness through objective function\n        2. Perform tournament selection with replacement\n        3. Apply crossover with crossover probability = 0.9\n        4. Apply mutation with mutation_rate probability and repair\n        5. Maintain best solutions across generations\n   - After sufficient generations, perform local search refinement\n   - Key operations are repair, crossover, mutation, and selection\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   - Use GA population size = 20\n   - Crossover probability = 0.9\n   - Mutation probability = 0.1\n   - Repair runs per operation = 200\n   - Local search temperature schedule = 300 steps with log cooling\n   - Minimum n_points = 560\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   - Repair: O(n) per solution, tractable for n=560\n   - Evaluation: O(n\u00b2) for correlation, but done on full resolution\n   - Selection: O(population_size) per generation\n   - Cooldown: 300 steps for cooling reduction\n   - Fallback: Return best solution found before timeout\n   - Backup repair: Enforce formulation consistently\n   - Single threaded execution\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   - Ensure 0 <= h[i] <= 1 and sum(h) = n/2 for all solutions\n   - Return best solution (h_values) and C5 value\n   - Verify fall back remains valid while exploring\n   - Re-verify solution satisfies all constraints\n   - Return standard (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)",
